theory.intro:
Each inverse trigonometric graph is the reflection of a restricted trigonometric graph in the line $y=x$. Reflecting swaps every point $(a,b)$ to $(b,a)$, so the restricted domain becomes the range and the restricted range becomes the domain.

[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[every node/.style={font=\large}, scale=1.4]
\draw[->] (-2.1,0) -- (2.1,0) node[right] {$x$};
\draw[->] (0,-2.1) -- (0,2.25) node[above] {$y$};
\draw[dashed] (-1.95,-1.95) -- (1.95,1.95);
\draw[thick,domain=-1.5708:1.5708,samples=80,smooth,variable=\t] plot ({\t},{sin(deg(\t))});
\draw[thick,blue,domain=-1.5708:1.5708,samples=80,smooth,variable=\t] plot ({sin(deg(\t))},{\t});
\fill (1.5708,1) circle (1.6pt);
\fill (-1.5708,-1) circle (1.6pt);
\fill[blue] (1,1.5708) circle (1.6pt);
\fill[blue] (-1,-1.5708) circle (1.6pt);
\draw (1,0.07) -- (1,-0.07);
\node[below,font=\small] at (1,-0.07) {$1$};
\draw (-1,0.07) -- (-1,-0.07);
\node[below,font=\small] at (-1,-0.07) {$-1$};
\draw (0.07,1.5708) -- (-0.07,1.5708);
\node[left,font=\small] at (-0.1,1.5708) {$\frac{\pi}{2}$};
\draw (0.07,-1.5708) -- (-0.07,-1.5708);
\node[left,font=\small] at (-0.1,-1.5708) {$-\frac{\pi}{2}$};
\node[font=\small] at (1.95,0.6) {$y=\sin x$};
\node[font=\small,blue] at (-0.95,2.0) {$y=\arcsin x$};
\node[font=\small] at (-1.25,-1.95) {$y=x$};
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]

workedExamples.solution_text:
Reflecting in $y=x$ swaps each point's coordinates. The endpoints become $\left(-1,-\frac{\pi}{2}\right)$ and $\left(1,\frac{\pi}{2}\right)$, and the requested point becomes $\left(-\frac12,-\frac{\pi}{6}\right)$.

Domain: $[-1,1]$. Range: $\left[-\frac{\pi}{2},\frac{\pi}{2}\right]$. Both endpoints are included, and there are no asymptotes. The curve is increasing and symmetric about the origin, so the function is odd.

[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[x=19mm,y=15mm,every node/.style={text=black,font=\fontsize{10}{12}\selectfont}]
\definecolor{curveblue}{HTML}{268CFF}
\draw[->,black] (-1.55,0)--(1.55,0) node[right] {$x$};
\draw[->,black] (0,-1.95)--(0,2.0) node[above] {$y$};
\draw[thick,curveblue,domain=-1.5707963268:1.5707963268,samples=101,variable=\t] plot ({sin(deg(\t))},{\t});
\fill[curveblue] (-1,-1.5707963268) circle (1.5pt);
\fill[curveblue] (1,1.5707963268) circle (1.5pt);
\fill[curveblue] (-0.5,-0.5235987756) circle (1.5pt);
\node[anchor=north east] at (-0.58,-0.80) {$\left(-\frac12,-\frac{\pi}{6}\right)$};
\foreach \a in {-1,1}{\draw[black] (\a,-0.05)--(\a,0.05);\node[below,font=\fontsize{8.5}{10}\selectfont] at (\a,-0.05) {$\a$};}
\draw[black] (-0.04,1.5707963268)--(0.04,1.5707963268);
\node[left,font=\fontsize{8.5}{10}\selectfont] at (-0.04,1.5707963268) {$\frac{\pi}{2}$};
\draw[black] (-0.04,-1.5707963268)--(0.04,-1.5707963268);
\node[right,font=\fontsize{8.5}{10}\selectfont] at (0.04,-1.5707963268) {$-\frac{\pi}{2}$};
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]

Reflect $y=\cos x$ for $0\le x\le\pi$ in $y=x$. The endpoints become $(1,0)$ and $(-1,\pi)$; $\left(\frac{\pi}{2},0\right)$ becomes $\left(0,\frac{\pi}{2}\right)$. The requested point becomes $\left(-\frac12,\frac{2\pi}{3}\right)$.

Domain: $[-1,1]$. Range: $[0,\pi]$. Both endpoints are included, and there are no asymptotes. The curve is decreasing. It has neither origin symmetry nor symmetry about the $y$-axis, so the function is neither odd nor even.

[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[x=20mm,y=14mm,every node/.style={text=black,font=\fontsize{10}{12}\selectfont}]
\definecolor{curveblue}{HTML}{268CFF}
\draw[->,black] (-1.65,0)--(1.55,0) node[right] {$x$};
\draw[->,black] (0,-0.4)--(0,3.65) node[above] {$y$};
\draw[thick,curveblue,domain=0:3.1415926536,samples=101,variable=\t] plot ({cos(deg(\t))},{\t});
\fill[curveblue] (-1,3.1415926536) circle (1.5pt);
\fill[curveblue] (1,0) circle (1.5pt);
\fill[curveblue] (-0.5,2.0943951024) circle (1.5pt);
\node[anchor=east] at (-0.58,1.98) {$\left(-\frac12,\frac{2\pi}{3}\right)$};
\foreach \a in {-1,1}{\draw[black] (\a,-0.05)--(\a,0.05);\node[below,font=\fontsize{8.5}{10}\selectfont] at (\a,-0.05) {$\a$};}
\draw[black] (-0.04,1.5707963268)--(0.04,1.5707963268);
\node[right,font=\fontsize{8.5}{10}\selectfont] at (0.04,1.5707963268) {$\frac{\pi}{2}$};
\draw[black] (-0.04,3.1415926536)--(0.04,3.1415926536);
\node[right,font=\fontsize{8.5}{10}\selectfont] at (0.04,3.1415926536) {$\pi$};
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]

Reflection changes the vertical asymptotes $x=\pm\frac{\pi}{2}$ into horizontal asymptotes $y=\pm\frac{\pi}{2}$.

The curve increases through the origin and approaches both asymptotes without reaching them. Domain: all real numbers. Range: $-\frac{\pi}{2}<y<\frac{\pi}{2}$.

Since $\tan\left(-\frac{\pi}{4}\right)=-1$ and $-\frac{\pi}{4}$ lies in the principal range, $\arctan(-1)=-\frac{\pi}{4}$. Mark $\left(-1,-\frac{\pi}{4}\right)$.

[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[x=8mm,y=17mm,every node/.style={text=black,font=\fontsize{10}{12}\selectfont}]
\definecolor{curveblue}{HTML}{268CFF}
\draw[->,black] (-4.2,0)--(4.2,0) node[right] {$x$};
\draw[->,black] (0,-2.05)--(0,2.05) node[above] {$y$};
\draw[dashed,black] (-4,1.5707963268)--(4,1.5707963268);
\draw[dashed,black] (-4,-1.5707963268)--(4,-1.5707963268);
\node[above] at (-2.3,1.5707963268) {$y=\frac{\pi}{2}$};
\node[below] at (2.3,-1.5707963268) {$y=-\frac{\pi}{2}$};
\draw[thick,curveblue,<->,domain=-4:4,samples=121,variable=\t] plot ({\t},{rad(atan(\t))});
\fill[curveblue] (-1,-0.7853981634) circle (1.5pt);
\node[anchor=east] at (-1.18,-0.65) {$\left(-1,-\frac{\pi}{4}\right)$};
\draw[black] (-1,-0.04)--(-1,0.04);
\node[above,font=\fontsize{8.5}{10}\selectfont] at (-1,0.04) {$-1$};
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]

The arcsin curve increases from $\left(-\frac12,-\frac{\pi}{6}\right)$ through $(0,0)$ to $\left(\frac12,\frac{\pi}{6}\right)$. The arccos curve decreases from $\left(-\frac12,\frac{2\pi}{3}\right)$ through $\left(0,\frac{\pi}{2}\right)$ to $\left(\frac12,\frac{\pi}{3}\right)$.

[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[x=36mm,y=17mm,every node/.style={text=black,font=\fontsize{10}{12}\selectfont}]
\definecolor{curveblue}{HTML}{268CFF}
\definecolor{curvered}{HTML}{EF6068}
