\draw[black] (0.5,-0.04)--(0.5,0.04);
\node[below,font=\fontsize{8.5}{10}\selectfont] at (0.5,-0.04) {$\frac12$};
\draw[black] (-0.025,1.5707963268)--(0.025,1.5707963268);
\node[left,font=\fontsize{8.5}{10}\selectfont] at (-0.025,1.5707963268) {$\frac{\pi}{2}$};
\node[text=curveblue,anchor=west] at (0.06,0.68) {$y=\arcsin x$};
\node[text=curvered,anchor=west] at (0.06,2.3) {$y=\arccos x$};
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]

Add the two heights at each $x$. At the left endpoint, centre and right endpoint the sums are respectively $-\frac{\pi}{6}+\frac{2\pi}{3}=\frac{\pi}{2}$, $0+\frac{\pi}{2}=\frac{\pi}{2}$ and $\frac{\pi}{6}+\frac{\pi}{3}=\frac{\pi}{2}$.

The complementary principal angles have sum $\frac{\pi}{2}$ throughout the interval. The sum graph is therefore the horizontal segment $y=\frac{\pi}{2}$ for $-\frac12\le x\le\frac12$, with closed endpoints.

